<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="num-ratio-proportion">
  <title>Ratio and Proportion</title>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Outcomes</title>
    <ul>
      <li><xref ref="lo-n-9" /> — Ratio and proportion</li>
      <li><xref ref="lo-u-11" /> — Relevance of mathematics</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <p>
    A <term>ratio</term> compares two quantities of the same kind.
    The ratio <m>3:2</m> means for every 3 of one thing, there are 2 of another.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ratios can be simplified like fractions: <m>12:8 = 3:2</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <term>Proportion</term> means two ratios are equal: <m>\dfrac{a}{b} = \dfrac{c}{d}</m>.
    We use proportion to find unknown values when quantities scale together.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Example:</strong> If 5 pens cost €3.50, how much do 8 pens cost?
  </p>
  <p>
    <m>\dfrac{5}{3.50} = \dfrac{8}{x} \Rightarrow x = \dfrac{8 \times 3.50}{5} = €5.60</m>
  </p>

  <exercises xml:id="exs-ratio">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ratio-simplify">
      <statement><p>Simplify the ratio <m>24:36</m>.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>2:3</m></p></answer>
      <solution><p>HCF of 24 and 36 is 12. So <m>24:36 = 2:3</m>.</p></solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ratio-share">
      <statement><p>Share €120 in the ratio <m>3:5</m>.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>€45 and €75</p></answer>
      <solution><p>Total parts: <m>3 + 5 = 8</m>. One part: <m>120 \div 8 = €15</m>. Shares: <m>3 \times 15 = €45</m> and <m>5 \times 15 = €75</m>.</p></solution>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</section>
